            <HouseholdOperatingConditions numberOfOccupants="2" coolingSeasonMonths="0" isClothesWasherEnergySaver="true" isLightingEnergySaver="true" areAppliancesEnergySaver="false">
                <Thermostat heatingDaytime="19" heatingNighttime="19" cooling="25" />
            </HouseholdOperatingConditions>
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="1" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="0" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house is well-sealed, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. I observed a lot of air leakage around all three doors to your house, including the one to your garage. 



I also checked around the windows you replaced recently, and I did not note any significant air leakage around them. 



I did also note some air leakage through your bathroom fan, and your range hood. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STARÂ® rated model. Your range hood was not working during your evaluation, and I recommend replacing this as the range hood is designed to vent cooking odours outside, and this will affect your indoor air quality.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $185&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;5.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 2x2 strapping with R5 insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation to all of the exterior walls, and to the common wall between your house and garage.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R15 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $875&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;25.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <windows>

As you have already started replacing your windows, and have indicated an interest in upgrading the rest of your windows, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

You have already upgraded 3 windows. By replacing the remaining 6 windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $110&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem />
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired boiler was originally rated at 83% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency natural gas boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency boiler (direct vented out a side wall), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion, gas-fired boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $375&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;37.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



Because your hot water comes off of your boiler, this upgrade will also increase the efficiency of your hot water system, saving an additional $5 and 3.6 GJ per year. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



I have also modelled the savings if you were to upgrade from your current oil boiler to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency oil boiler with a minimum efficiency of 87% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency oil boiler (direct vented out a side wall), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion, oil-fired boiler with an efficiency of 87% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $475&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;14.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



Because your hot water comes off of your boiler, this upgrade will also increase the efficiency of your hot water, resulting in further savings of about $20 and 0.7 GJ per year.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ÂlearnÂ the systemÂs usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house you can qualify for &lt;b&gt;$ 175 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Inuvik&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Inuvik:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.31 per kWh

Natural gas - $37.21 per GJ

Oil  - $1.21 per litre 

Propane - $1.52 per litre

Wood - $472.50 per cord 

Pellets - $11.25 per bag



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



